---
id: AP-017
type: rule
family: dynamic-execution
title: Server-side template injection (dynamic template string)
version: 1

severity: error
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 3

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.py, .js, .ts, .jsx, .tsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist:
  [
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/test_*.py',
    '**/*_test.py',
    '**/tests/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '(?:render_template_string|\.from_string|nunjucks\.renderString)\s*\(\s*(?:[A-Za-z_$`]|[''"][^''"]*[''"]\s*\+)'

related: [AP-008, AP-009]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

You're compiling a template from a dynamic string. Server-side template
injection (SSTI, CWE-1336) is the template-engine cousin of `eval`: Jinja,
Nunjucks, and friends expose object attributes and callables from inside
the template, so an attacker who controls the *template source* — not just
the data — can reach `__globals__` / `constructor` and pivot to arbitrary
code execution.

The first character inside the call is an identifier (a variable, call, or
f-string), which means the template body is not a static literal you
control. Render a **fixed** template and pass untrusted values as *data*
instead: `render_template('page.html', name=user_name)` /
`nunjucks.render('page.njk', { name })`. The engine escapes data by
contract; it does not escape a template you hand it.

If you genuinely need user-authored templates, run them in a sandboxed
environment (Jinja's `SandboxedEnvironment`, a locked-down Nunjucks
sandbox) and treat that surface as a security boundary. A *pure* static
literal argument (`render_template_string('<h1>Static</h1>')`) is skipped —
the leading character inside the parens is a quote — but a literal that is
then concatenated (`render_template_string('Hi ' + name)`) still fires,
since that is the literal-prefixed SSTI shape.
